{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Roasted with fire, and unleavened bread.</b> The manner of roasting is whether on a spit, or over coals, or in an oven heated to the utmost — and similar methods were permitted; but roasted in water, which they call רושט\"א, is forbidden, and this is what is written: ובשל מבושל במים — and all the more so if it was cooked in the liquids themselves, which is forbidden. Now the Torah forbade eating it נא (raw) or מבושל במים (cooked in water), but only צלי אש — roasted over the fire, whether on a spit or over coals or in a hot oven — and to exclude a vessel, meaning to place it in a vessel and roast it in it, is forbidden; unlike the rest of the consecrated offerings (קדשים), which are eaten whether roasted or cooked. And what our Rabbis of blessed memory said, that priestly gifts (מתנות כהונה) are eaten roasted because it is said למשחה — like the way kings eat — the verse speaks only of a preferred practice (מעלה בעלמא), that they should choose for themselves fine eating, and this is צלי אש; but if a priest wished to eat them whether roasted or cooked, the prerogative is in his hand — unlike the Paschal offering (פסח), which may not be eaten cooked but only roasted, and this is what the Mishnah stated: אינו נאכל אלא צלי. And what is written (Deuteronomy 16:6): תזבח את הפסח בערב וגו׳ ובשלת ואכלת — this is not cooking in water but cooking in fire itself, in the manner of what is written about Josiah (2 Chronicles 35:13): ויבשלו הפסח באש, according to the law (כמשפט) where the intent is not cooking in a vessel, in the manner of what is written (ibid.): והקדשים בשלו בסירות ובדודים ובצלחות. From this you learn that the cooking of the Paschal offering is צלי אש, and in Old French אשאונא\"ר, from the language of (Genesis 40:10) הבשילו אשכלותיה ענבים, (Joel 4:13) כי בשל קציר — and the intent is not roasting with fire in a vessel." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> צלי אש — in the merit of Abraham, whom I saved from the fire. מצות — in the merit of Sarah's three cakes. על מרורים — just as his sons were pursued in Egypt, so too Esau pursued them. לא תותירו — just as no soul remained among the firstborn of Egypt, so too you shall not leave over from the roast. מתניכם חגורים, נעליכם ברגליכם — like people hastening to go." "<b>And you should know</b> that the passage here concerning the Paschal offering itself was a commandment for that time and for all generations; but מתניכם חגורים and ודם על המשקוף — this is not in the Paschal offering for all generations but only in the Egyptian Paschal offering alone."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:08.247870497Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}